\section{The rotation stopping rule}{
Only \code{\link[=fcast_dfm]{fcast_dfm()}} rotates, so \verb{rotation_*} is ignored by \code{\link[=ind_dfm]{ind_dfm()}}.

Appendix E of the online appendix to Eckert et al. (2025), following Assmann,
Boysen-Hogrefe & Pape (2016), specifies convergence when the \strong{sum} of
squared deviations between successive \verb{theta*} falls below \code{1e-9}. The
implementation has always tested the \strong{mean}, which over a packed vector of
several thousand elements is a much weaker requirement, and additionally
capped the loop at five iterations. \code{rotation_criterion = "sum"} restores the
published rule.

Measured on a small two-factor fit, convergence is geometric at roughly an
order of magnitude per iteration: the mean criterion converged at iteration 5
and the sum criterion at iteration 6. Matching the paper therefore costs
about one extra iteration, not the blow-up the difference in thresholds
suggests. Note also that the default cap of 5 sits \emph{exactly} on that
convergence point, so on other data it can bind and truncate the loop -- which
is why a binding cap warns.

\code{rotation_max_iter} and \code{rotation_init_max_iter} are safety valves, not
targets. They default high enough not to bind in practice (100 against 5-7
observed) while still guaranteeing the loop terminates. \code{Inf} is not accepted:
an unbounded loop has no termination guarantee, and a non-converging rotation
should stop and complain rather than run forever.
}

\section{Stability bounds}{
\code{rho_max} bounds the measurement-error autocorrelation. A draw outside it is
redrawn up to \code{rho_max_tries} times, after which \code{rho_fallback} is used. The
others cap or reject a draw for numerical stability: \code{phi_sum_max} and
\code{sigma_max} in \code{\link[=ind_dfm]{ind_dfm()}}, \code{omega_max} in \code{\link[=fcast_dfm]{fcast_dfm()}}.
}

\section{Retaining draws}{
Both samplers hold every retained draw in memory for the whole fit anyway;
until #110 they were averaged and discarded when the sampler returned, so a
fit carried posterior means and no chain. These settings decide what survives
into the fit object, not what is computed -- nothing here consumes RNG, and
the numbers a fit reports are identical with them on or off.

\describe{
\item{\code{keep_draws}}{Default \code{TRUE}. Stores the parameter and nowcast draws
in \code{fit$draws} (see \link{mfbdfm_draws}), which is what
\code{\link[=mfbdfm_diagnostics]{mfbdfm_diagnostics()}} and the trace plots read. Small: one row per
retained draw and one column per scalar parameter, plus one column per
nowcast period -- a few hundred kilobytes for a typical chain.}
\item{\code{keep_factor_draws}}{Default \code{FALSE}, because this is the one large
component: \verb{length_sample x (t+s) x q} doubles, which at 1000 draws of a
weekly 30-year sample is of the order of 10 MB per factor. On for the
posterior of any functional of the factor path.}
\item{\code{keep_burn_in}}{Default \code{FALSE}, \strong{\code{\link[=ind_dfm]{ind_dfm()}} only}. Retains the
burn-in \emph{parameter} draws as well, so a trace plot can show the chain
settling rather than starting at the first kept draw.}
}

\code{keep_burn_in} is absent from \code{\link[=fcast_dfm]{fcast_dfm()}} deliberately, rather than
accepted and ignored. That model identifies post hoc: a draw is comparable
with other draws only after the rotation step has mapped it onto a
reference computed from the \emph{retained} draws, and a burn-in draw has no such
reference. Rotating the burn-in too would either move that reference -- and so
change the results -- or cost an extra optimisation per burn-in draw, while
storing it unrotated would put a non-comparable series on a trace plot. Naming
a setting the chosen model does not have is an error, as with \code{phi_sum_max}
and \code{omega_max}; the parity rule is about the same \emph{concept} in both models,
and this concept does not exist there.
}

\section{Verbosity}{
\code{verbose = FALSE} silences both the progress \code{message()}s and the
\code{utils::txtProgressBar}. The progress bar writes with \code{cat()}, so
\code{suppressMessages()} alone does not quieten a fit -- which is why this is a
setting rather than something a caller can arrange from outside. Use it for
scripted sweeps and for the worker processes planned by \code{\link[=dfm_workers]{dfm_workers()}}.
}

\section{Muffling one warning but not the others}{
The warnings a fit can raise repeatedly over a sweep carry their own
condition classes, so a single kind can be suppressed without hiding the
rest:

\describe{
\item{\code{mfbdfm_warning_rho_fallback}}{the measurement-error autocorrelation
hit \code{rho_max_tries} redraws for at least one series and \code{rho_fallback}
was substituted. Raised once per fit, with the number of substitutions.}
\item{\code{mfbdfm_warning_rotation_cap}}{the \code{\link[=fcast_dfm]{fcast_dfm()}} rotation (or its
initialisation) stopped on its iteration cap rather than on convergence.}
\item{\code{mfbdfm_warning_fit_failed}}{a fit failed and
\code{\link[=run_fcast]{run_fcast()}}\code{(on_error = "warn")} turned the error into a warning.}
\item{\code{mfbdfm_warning_collinear}}{two or more input series are
near-perfectly correlated on their overlapping observed span. Raised
once per fit, naming the pairs. Muffle it once you have decided the
duplication is intended -- see the "Near-collinear input series"
section of \code{\link[=mfbdfm_data]{mfbdfm_data()}} for what it costs you if it is not.}
}

All of them also inherit from \code{mfbdfm_warning}. To muffle one:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{withCallingHandlers(
  fcast_dfm(flows = flows, stocks = stocks, target = target),
  mfbdfm_warning_rho_fallback = function(w) invokeRestart("muffleWarning")
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Substituting \code{mfbdfm_warning} for the class name muffles all of them.
}

